Well, exactly when which rules apply is probably an important issue.  We kn=
ow that pronoun rules are late and deprenexification is fairly early, but j=
ust what comes where is going to depend on best ways to set limits.  The cu=
rrent grammar is probably a target for the deeper layers, but, as it stands=
, it seems to have lost some essential pieces, so that it looks like the sa=
me sentence may come from more than one nonequivalent formula even though i=
t has only one parse.  The easiest cases seem to come from deprenexificatio=
n, where scope is not specified (can't find my list at the moment).

